  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was a mixed day across both locations. Port Washington ran hot on a per-groomer basis\u2014$532/groomer against a 30-day baseline of $465\u2014but transaction count dropped 11% and core grooming revenue fell 6% week-over-week, suggesting a softer Monday post-holiday rather than execution strength. The bright spot: retail jumped 27%, and add-on attach improved to 67%, your highest leverage metric. Hicksville reversed course dramatically, up 27% in grooming revenue with only two groomers on the floor, though that same capacity constraint means add-on attach cratered to 20%\u2014Julia and Julianne are booked solid but not upselling. Both stores face a structural headwind: September opened with zero appointments month-to-date, which is calendar-driven (Labor Day landed Sunday this year, shifting the typical Monday-Tuesday traffic), not performance loss. Your DOW-adjusted expectation for Port was $3.5K; you're at zero through today, which is expected given the holiday footprint.\n\nThe operational signal worth attention: Hicksville's add-on collapse (20% attach vs. 40% last week) suggests your team there is survival-mode full, not in a position to layer upsells. Port's retail strength masks a visit-cadence concern\u2014you've got 505 lapsed/drifting customers at Port representing $318K in annual value. That's reactivation fuel if you have capacity to chase it.\n\nYou should be proud of Port's retail execution and margin mix discipline in a down-transaction week.\n\nToday: Port Washington 5 groomers, 0 bather hours; Hicksville 3 groomers, 0 bather hours. Next 7 days: 52 combined groomer-days, 76 bather hours (all Port), which sets you up to absorb the post-Labor Day rebound starting midweek."
